# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real chat data must never be committed.
#
# The stress corpora under data/ are SYNTHETIC and stay tracked. Anything derived from a real
# WeChat account is not, and the safe convention is to keep it in `data/real/` (ignored entirely)
# or outside the repository altogether.
#
# What counts as real and sensitive here:
#   * raw WeFlow JSON exports — message bodies, wxids, serverIds
#   * converted TXT corpora built from real chats (the canonical form is still real content)
#   * WeChat message databases and anything decrypted out of them
#   * the 3.x decrypt key, or any file that carries it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# real corpora, by location
data/real/
.local/
private/

# the persistent retrieval index. It is derived from real chat — vectors AND the session text they
# point at — so it is exactly as sensitive as the export it was built from. It is also derived: the
# JSON export tree is still the source of truth, so losing it costs one rebuild and nothing else.
.index_cache/

# real corpora, by name (so an accidental export in data/ is still caught)
real_*.txt
my_chat*.txt
my_export*.txt
*_export.json
weflow*.json
*.weflow.json

# Named after the actual files a real smoke test produced locally — these were found sitting
# untracked and NOT ignored, one `git add -A` away from committing real chat lines.
wechat*.txt
wechat_*.json
*_smoke.txt
smoke_*.txt
recall_debug*.json
*_debug.json

# WeChat databases and their derivatives
MSG*.db
MSG*.db-*
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# key material — never in the repo, never in a log
*.key
*_key.txt
wechat_key*
decrypt_key*

# local environment
.env
.env.*

# scratch produced by offline tests
tests/_scratch_*/
